            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CAM4",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "HAS_SUBSEQUENT_VERSION",
            "target_entity_instance": "CAM5",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "Firstly, CAM4 has been replaced by CAM5, which includes updates in the cloud microphysics and dynamics schemes. CAM5 resolves a previously identified bias in CAM4, namely the excessive formation of liquid precipitation at sub-zero temperatures over the ice sheets (Vizca\u00edno et al. 2013).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Community Earth System Model (CESM), version 1.1.2",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "coupled",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "In this study we use the coupled version of the Community Earth System Model (CESM), version 1.1.2.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Community Earth System Model (CESM), version 1.1.2",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "snow capping scheme",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "To impose mass conservation within the framework of the global model, additional snowfall above the maximum snow thickness is immediately routed into the nearest ocean grid point, while at the same time being recorded as a positive mass flux. This method represents an ice sheet in mass balance, where mass input via SMB is balanced by equivalent ice calving at the ice sheet margin. Such schemes are common to many contemporary climate models, and implicitly assume an ice sheet that is in long-term mass balance, because on an annual basis, the excess mass routed to the ocean via the snow capping scheme is approximately equal to the mass of snow falling on the ice sheet.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Community Earth System Model (CESM), version 1.1.2",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "five snow layers",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "CLM contains a maximum of five layers of snow that can collectively contain up to one metre water equivalent (w.e.) of snow.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Community Earth System Model (CESM), version 1.1.2",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "constant Antarctic Ice Sheet topography",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "In the absence of a robust model to realistically simulate ice sheet-shelf interactions, Antarctic ice flow is not yet incorporated in CESM, unlike for Greenland (Lipscomb et al. 2013), and AIS topography is kept constant for the course of the simulation.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "atmospheric rivers",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "East Antarctica",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "In recent years, parts of East Antarctica have been characterised by unusually frequent passages of strong short-termed precipitation events, so-called atmospheric rivers (Gorodetskaya et al. 2014); it is unclear however, whether this is due to natural variability or a manifestation of warming (Lenaerts et al. 2013).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Community Earth System Model (CESM)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Surface Mass Balance",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "We present climate and surface mass balance (SMB) of the Antarctic ice sheet (AIS) as simulated by the global, coupled ocean-atmosphere-land Community Earth System Model (CESM) with a horizontal resolution of ~1\u00b0 in the past, present and future (1850\u20132100).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Community Earth System Model (CESM)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "sea ice extent",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "CESM correctly simulates present-day Antarctic sea ice extent, large-scale atmospheric circulation and near-surface climate, but fails to simulate the recent expansion of Antarctic sea ice.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Community Earth System Model (CESM)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "near-surface climate",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "CESM correctly simulates present-day Antarctic sea ice extent, large-scale atmospheric circulation and near-surface climate, but fails to simulate the recent expansion of Antarctic sea ice.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "RACMO2",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Surface Mass Balance",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Integrated over AIS (Table 1, including ice shelves), contemporary CESM SMB equals 2290 \u00b1 131 Gt year\u00af\u00b9, which is lower than the RACMO2 SMB (2596\u00b1121 Gt year\u00af\u00b9), but agrees with other, independent estimates within the uncertainties (Vaughan et al. 1999; Lenaerts et al. 2012a).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
